---
layout: recipe
title: Asparagus and Lemon Orzotto
description: Creamy risotto-style orzo with asparagus, peas, spinach, lemon and crème fraîche. One pan and about half an hour, with a fraction of a risotto's stirring.
preview: Risotto-style orzo with asparagus, peas and spinach, brightened with lemon and finished with crème fraîche.
intro: |
  All of the comfort of risotto with a fraction of the babysitting. Orzo does the whole risotto trick — swelling, releasing starch, turning silky — without demanding constant attention. Keep a pan of stock on the hob, add it a ladleful at a time, and stir only occasionally: between stirs, trim the asparagus and zest the lemon, and you'll be at the table in about half an hour.

  The crème fraîche is what quiets the lemon and makes the pan look creamy; the parmesan is doing the salt. Chicken stock works just as well as vegetable if the fridge isn't watching.
note: Leftovers reheat well with a splash of hot water and another spoonful of crème fraîche.
---

Bring the @vegetable stock{1200%ml} to a bare simmer in a #small saucepan{} and keep it warm over a low heat.

Heat the @olive oil{2%tbsp} in a #deep frying pan{} over a medium heat. Add the @shallot{1}, finely chopped, and cook until soft but not coloured, ~{3-4%minutes}.

Add the @garlic{2%cloves}, finely chopped, and cook for ~{1%minute} more.

Stir in the @orzo{300%g} and toast, stirring, for ~{1-2%minutes} until it smells faintly nutty. Not strictly required but very good: pour in a splash of @white wine{100%ml} and let it bubble away.

Add the first ladleful of hot stock and stir until absorbed. Keep going, ladleful by ladleful, stirring every couple of minutes and letting each addition be absorbed before the next.

After about ~{10%minutes}, with most of the stock incorporated but the pan not yet dry, work in the @asparagus{300%g} — woody ends trimmed, cut into short lengths — and the @frozen peas{150%g}. Cook for ~{4-5%minutes} more, until the orzo has a tender bite and the pan is dry once again.

Fold in the @baby spinach{100%g} in two or three batches, letting each batch wilt before adding the next.

Take the pan off the heat and finish it: stir in the zest and juice of @lemon{1}, the @crème fraîche{3%tbsp}, and the @parmesan{40%g}, finely grated. Season generously with salt and black pepper. If it looks tight, loosen it with a final splash of hot stock — the orzotto should flow lazily across the pan, not sit in a heap.

Serve in warm bowls topped with extra parmesan, a little more lemon zest and plenty of black pepper.
